<!--Post: 12054--><!--Time: 1111537063-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12054"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 4:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It was one of the choices along with BTK for a media name submitted by the killer and he wrote poetry in his communications but what is suspect Dennis Rader&#39;s connection to poetry? Why the interest in poetry? Did he take classes in poetry at WSU.? In high school? Where did this interest come from? <BR>I&#39;ve heard of no one linking Dennis Rader to an interest in poetry except for the questionable story of the KFC employee in the National Enquirer.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12054-->
<!--Post: 12068--><!--Time: 1111546590-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12068"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 6:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Good point, Johno.  The poetry mystery has only deepened. <BR> <BR>Frankly, the NE has been looking pretty good on crime items since they started scooping the negligent mainstream press on scandalous political stuff.  My money rides on it being true.  I think he was hanging at KFC, pumped up by his own media blitz, hoping to relive his glory days, but ran into the S.K. equivalent of E.D.  The &#34;killer moves&#34; were gone, so to speak - and thank goodness. <BR> <BR>Yes, it seems that P.J. Wyatt has vanished into the mists of time.  No A Blackout.  No beat poetry.  No artistic temperment - not counting the fact that the guy didn&#39;t crack a joke in his entire high school experience &#40;what kind of artist is that?&#41;.  Why hasn&#39;t somebody else produced a screwy Rader love poem?  Maybe all this stuff is just being dumped on LE and they&#39;re keeping mum.  But I would think that some of it would be turning up on the news. <BR> <BR>This isn&#39;t the only thing that&#39;s bothering me.  I have yet to hear of circumstantial evidence putting him through the door to one of his unwitnessed crimes.  Even that aspect of his life is wanting for detail - and that is arguably the most important part for LE right now.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12068-->
<!--Post: 12069--><!--Time: 1111547972-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12069"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 7:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I once thought that it wasn&#39;t poetry in the classical sense of poems that his interest was in but rather song lyrics that inspired his interest in poetry. Then I read that 25th 8th grade reunion questionaire where they asked his favorite songs of the era and he replied that he had no interest in music at that time in his life. I don&#39;t think he ever developed any musical interests on his own except for hymns that he undoubtably would have been exposed to in a Lutheran congregation. <BR>That&#39;s weird that somebody who has no interest in music would be an aspiring poet.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12069-->
<!--Post: 12075--><!--Time: 1111553235-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12075"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 8:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I know this may sound strange, but what if Rader wrote poetry because he HATED it?  What if his intention was more to mock it - driven by his hatred of women and in turn his hatred for something that &#40;stereotypically&#41; he thought they would love?  Instead of bringing poetry and chocolates, he brought mock poems and death. <BR> <BR>So instead of &#34;On Desire&#34;, written by a woman, the woman-hater BTK turns it on its head, creating something that might as well have been entitled &#34;On Sexual Sadism&#34;. <BR> <BR>Based on this theory, the reason he&#39;s a mediocre poet is actually that he is a far-better-than-mediocre mocker and satirist &#40;although not at the level of even a weekend comedian or gag writer&#41;.  He never wrote poetry outside of his BTK life because it was only part of his BTK persona, and then only as an act of hatred.  He never gave a rodent&#39;s posterior if it was any good. <BR> <BR>To borrow a phrase from steve_huff.....There never was a poet.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12075-->
<!--Post: 12077--><!--Time: 1111554239-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12077"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 9:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OMG, I just got the joke.  You can read &#34;poetic strangler&#34; two ways.  The second way, as in &#34;strangler of poems&#34; &#40;more like &#34;poem strangler&#34;&#41;, is the one I had not considered previously.  Perhaps the joke is on all of us.  If so, it would have been for about 30 years.  Holy Toledo.  We were looking for a poet, when we should have been looking for a poetry hater. <BR> <BR>Thank goodness for DNA.  This guy is slippery as a catfish in crisco.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12077-->
<!--Post: 12078--><!--Time: 1111554355-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12078"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 9:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Exactly Johno, he hated music! I wondered when I read that info on the Riverview Roundup, who doesn&#39;t like some  kind music? You know then I thought of what music was popular in that era. So, he would have been in middle school in the mid 60&#39;s right? Wasn&#39;t Bob Dylan just breaking out around then with folk lore music?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12078-->
<!--Post: 12079--><!--Time: 1111554473-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12079"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 9:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Was there ever a song written about Zodiac?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12079-->
<!--Post: 12083--><!--Time: 1111555607-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12083"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 9:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The &#34;Hair&#34; stuff &#40;Aquarius, etc.&#41; was as close to Zodiac as I can recall - late 60&#39;s, early 70&#39;s.  There was a fascination with things like that around that time.  I think people like me who were barely paying attention to Zodiac, simply assumed he was just being &#34;hip&#34; like everyone else in choosing that moniker.  Whether that&#39;s true or not is beyond me - a question for Johno.  Astrological topics were generally considered anti-establishment, long-hair stuff, but they gained popularity as people tried to be cool. <BR> <BR>The song lyrics thing about Rader convinces me that he wasn&#39;t a poet.  Song lyrics are poetry lite.  If you don&#39;t give a darn about lyrics, you can&#39;t honestly be into poetry, IMHO. <BR> <BR>OK, maybe he had some sudden musical awakening around the time he wrote the poems.  But that just seems a little far-fetched.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12083-->
<!--Post: 12087--><!--Time: 1111556664-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12087"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 9:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That or he dropped acid!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12087-->
<!--Post: 12088--><!--Time: 1111557483-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12088"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 9:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->LOL!!!  I don&#39;t know - I think Rader was probably strange enough that acid might have normalized him. <BR> <BR>Lord, just imagine Denny in a fringe leather vest over his ADT uniform, and wearing a big &#39;fro.  Buyin&#39; tabs of Blue Cheer off of them &#34;three dude&#34;. <BR> <BR>On second thought, maybe better leave that thought alone.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12088-->
<!--Post: 12089--><!--Time: 1111558483-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12089"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 10:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->LMAO! Pax - Now that would be a sight! The narcissist on acid! Seriously, I think I will research music in that time frame to see what would have been bouncing off his ear drums.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12089-->
<!--Post: 12090--><!--Time: 1111558727-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12090"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 10:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ok - I found this: <BR><a href="http://www.wgeneration.com/1960.html" target=_top>http://www.wgeneration.com/1960.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12090-->
<!--Post: 12091--><!--Time: 1111560178-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12091"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 10:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Good link.  Dylan was really influential, and my friends who liked him were very into his lyrics.  People who dug church-like music often went for Emerson, Lake, and Palmer.  Jimi Hendrix was too hard for most, but I loved him.  Beatles were king, however.  Very influential.  Many kids dug them as their first big band influence.  Maybe his wife got him into music.  She might have had a more normal taste in music and influenced him.  I think he was in high school during the early to mid 60s.  Lots of music in the military, too. <BR> <BR>It all depends upon exactly when the music bug hit him &#40;if ever&#41;.  That would be the key.  But if he ever became obsessive about any particular band, then you know he got into the lyrics, and then poetry is a reasonable possibility.  Music - gateway to poetry!  Let that be a warning to kids. <BR> <BR>Gotta crash, dude!  Back atcha tomorrow.  Pax out!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12091-->
<!--Post: 12093--><!--Time: 1111565119-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12093"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 23, 2005 - 12:05 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#39;Oh Anna Why Didn&#39;t You Appear&#39;. <BR>Subconciously Biblically inspired? <BR> <BR>There is only one Anna in the Bible and she is found in the book of Luke. A very old prophetess who never leaves the temple and prays there day and night &#40;ironically potential victim Anna was not home&#41;. Jesus is brought to the temple to be circumsized on the eighth day of his life &#40;number 8 was not&#41;. Anna gives a prophecy to the infants life. Another prophet is also in the temple at the same time by the name of Simeon &#40;Dennis Rader&#39;s grandfather&#39;s middle name was Simeon&#41;.  <BR>  <BR>Excerpts from The Song Of Solomon compared to excerpts from &#40;Oh Anna&#41;. <BR> <BR>Now also thy breasts shall be as clusters of the vine. <BR>&#40;My pleasure of entanglement, like new vines at night.&#41; <BR> <BR>There my mother brought thee forth: there she brought thee forth that bare thee. <BR>&#40;Fantasy springs forth.&#41; <BR> <BR>Honey and milk are under thy tongue, and the smell of thy garments is like the smell of Lebanon. <BR>&#40;I lay with sweet enrapture garments.&#41; <BR> <BR>My beloved has gone down into the garden, to the beds of spices. <BR>&#40;Bed of Spring moist grass.&#41; <BR> <BR>Clean before the sun. <BR>&#40;I am black because the sun looked upon me.&#41; <BR> <BR>Thine eyes like fishpools of Heshbon. <BR>&#40;Eyes so deep and clear.&#41; <BR> <BR>The word &#39;rapture&#39; is found twice in the Oh Anna poem and although the word rapture is not found in the Song of Solomon or anywhere else in the Bible it has become synonomous with Christianity and the fact that it appears more than once in a relatively short poem indicates that the author may have recently been reading about a subject discussing rapture or in a discussion or heard a sermon with it used or is a common word in the authors vocabulary. <BR>The word enrapture is found in the books Proverbs and Acts in the Bible.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12093-->
