<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12192"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 24, 2005 - 10:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, one thing we can be sure of is that Rader read Song of Solomon, probably multiple times.  It&#39;s short, it stands out in the Bible, and it would have appeal to Rader.  It had to have been covered multiple times in sermons he heard over the years.  It would be a wonder if he had never read it very closely at least once. <BR> <BR>My current pet version of Johno&#39;s theory is that the rooting in Song of Solomon is incestuous, no pun intended.  Not only would Rader have read it - so would the writer of almost any plagiarized or borrowed-from poem that sounds remotely like it, particularly if written by a practicing or professing Christian, and more so if written back in an earlier and more prudish era, when Song of Solomon was, literature-wise, &#34;safe sex&#34;.  So the idea that it comes from another, non-Biblical poem &#40;probably containing the word &#34;propension&#34;&#41;, still appeals to me, but I would strongly agree that any apparent originality on Rader&#39;s part should be first suspected to have come from Song of Solomon, or at least what he subconsciously remembered of it.  It is, quite simply, the only poetry that we can be virtually certain he read.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
