<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12267"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 9:14 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky, I think most Lutherans or followers of any of the various faiths under the umbrella of Christianity believe that forgiveness of their sins and a hereafter not in hell would be granted for asking for forgiveness to God in Christ&#39;s name and showing genuine remorse for things like saying bad things about people, adultry, stealing and even unpremeditated murder in an act of passion. <BR>You would have to be pretty warped to think that you can commit 30 years of premeditated multiple murder and torture and taunting and die with a clean concience and no consquences because you believed in Christ and did work for the church your whole life. <BR>This is exactly however what I think Dennis Rader felt. &#40;I can do whatever I like. God knows I believe in him and do his Son&#39;s work. All I have to do is confess my sins and ask for forgiveness and I&#39;ll be just fine.&#41; <BR> <BR>Some people belive that even a good dead done without God is a sin. Example you save someone&#39;s life but you did it without God in your heart or accepting praise for doing it without mentioning God then you are sinning. <BR> <BR>Some people look for loopholes. <BR>Confessing and acknowleging your sins taken to an extreme. Believing in the linitless ability of God to forgive. <BR> <BR>&#34;No sin will separate us from the Lamb even though we commit fornication and murder a thousand times a day. Do you think that the purchase price that was paid for the redemption of our sins by so great a Lamb is too small? Pray boldly-you too are a mighty sinner.&#34;...Martin Luther, letter to Phillip Melanchthon, August 1, 1521. <BR> <BR>&#34;I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked, but rather that they turn from their ways and live.&#34;...God, The Holy Bible, Ezekiel 33:10-11. <BR> <BR>Now for something really sick, I suspect Dennis Rader prayed over his victims bodies in an attempt to absolve him of sin.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
