<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12268"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 9:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Itty, I thought it was &#34;Louise&#34; that was crossed out?  In that case, this old song recorded by the Yardbirds and Eric Clapton might make some sense &#40;just the line about why don&#39;t you hurry home&#41;: <BR> <BR>&#40;By John Lee Hooker&#41; <BR> <BR>Oh Louise, yes you&#39;re the sweetest girl I know. <BR>Don&#39;t you know now Louise, you are the sweetest girl that I know. <BR>You made me run from Chicago <BR>Oh, to the Gulf of Mexico. <BR> <BR>Now you know Louise, <BR>She&#39;s like a rolling stone. <BR>Every time she leave a man, <BR>He has to grieve alone. <BR> <BR>Oh Louise, why don&#39;t you hurry home? <BR>You know, I never had no good loving, <BR>Oh Louise, since you&#39;ve been gone. <BR> <BR>Got a girl called Mamie, <BR>Treat me nice and kind. <BR>I don&#39;t care what she do <BR>Because Louise is on my mind. <BR> <BR>[Chorus] <BR> <BR>Louise ain&#39;t so good looking <BR>And her hair ain&#39;t red, <BR>But she cooks my breakfast <BR>And I bring it to my bed. <BR> <BR>[Chorus] <BR> <BR>Oh Louise, why don&#39;t you hurry home? <BR>Oh Louise, why don&#39;t you hurry home? <BR>You made me run from Chicago <BR>Oh, to the Gulf of Mexico.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
