<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12280"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 26, 2005 - 2:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Pax <BR> <BR>Maybe there is another poem based of Song of Solomen which contains the word &#34;propension&#34; that Denny copied his poem from. Being a copycat of a copycat would fit his &#34;propension&#34; to take character flaws to the radical edge. <BR> <BR>And in other noise: <BR> <BR>While I was checking out the rams head/goat head thing I, of course, got got engrossed once again in all the &#34;satan sites&#34;, particularly one which was written by a retired Naval Intelligence dude who specialized in the occult &#40;against it, not for it&#41; who was making the case that the Masons organization is actually an occult organization. <BR> <BR>DISCLAIMER: To be fair to the Masons and all related orgs., The symbols that they use that are overtly Occult are just that: OCCULT meaning unknown, and predate any real notion of heaven or hell or Satan &#40;except as a fallen angel, celestial troublemaker, and actually before the idea that he was the serpent in eden was adopted by what would become the Chistian and Jewish faiths&#41;. Occult symbols are basically symbols of &#34;mystery&#34; and have been used by anyone who practiced mysticism. <BR> <BR>What did strike me about the article, though was the familiar theme one runs into whenever you look seriously at satanism and the occult, and that it that it a hell of a lot of work and that most people think of evil as a &#34;shortcut&#34; around the rules. <BR> <BR>The first rule is that you use whoever you want to in order to achieve your personal objectives&#40; this is why there really is no &#34;organized world wide satanic organization like there is a world wide network of, say, the Christian Church &#41;. A satanic church is usually made up of one or two actual &#34;Satanist&#34; who have spent a great deal of time in big city libraries studying old books, and are not &#34;inheirantly evil&#34; enough to waste the knowledge on petty stuff like immediate personal gratification. Their &#34;congregation&#34; is made up of the aforementioned n&#39;erdowells who are looking for a short cut in life and are selfish enough, that they don&#39;t mind hurting other people to get what they want. They are led through the theatrics of some low level rituals and then used to to the dirty work until they get killed, incarcerated or smart. <BR> <BR>This was the guys veiw of the Masons by the way: that the top few percent were somehow related to the ancient Illuminati and that they used the &#34;social organization&#34; of the Masons to funnel the will of the Illuminati down to the &#34;street level&#34;. <BR> <BR> This is by no means a new idea...there have been accusations of this and that against the Masons since the Crusades and that debate has nothing to do with Rader other than illustrate this point: The Masons is an organization that is, at least loosly, based on some principles of mysticism and by nature contains &#34;truths within truths&#34; in the sense that Alice in Wonderland can be read as a childs adventure &#40; a sort of creepy one &#41; or an adult book about enlightenment of the intellect through psychotropic drugs &#40; also a bit creepy &#41;. In other words you get from it what you are capable of understanding. <BR> <BR>Mysticism uses the &#34;building block&#34; method of teaching where the learnee would be guided from the lower level to a higher level by organizing their input so that they are given specifically what they need in the way of factual information and then guided through an experience or ritual to require them to use it in order to make them functionally aware of the principle being taught. <BR> <BR>NOW, &#40;if you are even still bothering to read this&#41; WHAT DOES THIS HAVE TO DO WITH DENNIS RADER? <BR> <BR>Our man Denny, whether his Monster is real or not, definately entered adulthood with a demon or two. I think his involvement with the Christian Faith was a carryover from childhood and actually did help him with his day to day problems. Aside from his Secret life as BTK he had the same problems as anyone else in his life as Dennis Rader. But I don&#39;t believe he accepted God, Jesus, and the Church the same way that other church goin&#39; folk do...simply because it didn&#39;t help him with that nasty &#34;monster&#34; problem. His relationship to his pastor was and still is more like a guy and his Bartender. 99% of dealing with most everyday problems is just talking it out. But Denny had something more than just an everyday problem. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m not going to even try to guess exactly which screw was loose, but something was wrong by the time he left the military, for sure, whereas he was no longer relying on the Christian church for true spiritual guidance. When I say this I mean, if we can assume the &#34;Christ on the Cross&#34; posting is actually his, which I think everyone has,by the time he left the military he felt a need to kill and to &#34;preserve the experience fo everyday use&#34;. By then, he felt the full force the sado/sexual effect of killing &#40;in his own words&#41; and since the Christian Church makes no provision for such a selfish act, and assuming that any delusions that he held would have progressed over thirty years, rather than regressed, it is logical to assume that, if he held any spiritual beliefs at all he had abandoned the Christian Church. <BR> <BR>Being raised in the church I would think that he had a strong sense of himself in a relation to the world at large, and thus would be driven to some extent, to logically justify his place in the overall scheme of things. <BR> <BR>Since, of course, no religion actually condones murder for the sake of personal sexual gratification, he would be limited pretty much to the occult practices.  <BR> <BR>Exactly which kind, when and to what extent he ever became involved in the occult depends a lot on 1&#41;what made him want to need to kill, 2&#41;at what age he first wanted to need to kill and 3&#41;the circumstances that made him want to need to kill. <BR> <BR>For instance, was he possibly abused as a child, maybe by someone who believed in the occult? Was he enamored by some Oriental Mystic cult in the Air Force? Did he seek something out after he had killed, looking for justification?  <BR> <BR>I think, no matter what, he was not &#34;schooled&#34;, meaning not formally taken through a process. If he did dabble in Satanism, was too smart to be used, and didn&#39;t have the time or resources to research it all up. And he is egocentric enough to believe that his mind, with his &#34;novel thinking&#34; was capable of charting his way through the process by piecing the higher knowlege together using miscellaneous sources, over time. <BR> <BR>If, and I still haven&#39;t given this up completely, he was involved with others at the start, then I believe that he may have been somewhat interested in Satanism or the occult, but only in the sense that he would kill anyway and that made him &#34;head demon&#34;. If, and this looks more like a possibility all of the time, there is more of a family, and generational, dark side, then I think he might be guided by some archaic mystical beliefs that he never questioned. Or as has been suggested, he sees himself as part of part of an unofficial killers club, the symbol and the original ritual of motive and the &#34;name&#34; were all just part of being &#34;one of the guys&#34;. <BR> <BR>But all in all, and harking back to my earlier guestion: &#34;What was his success strategy?&#34;, I&#39;ve pretty much concluded he had none. <BR> <BR>I think he let his need for self gratification overpower his faith. I think that any attempt to delude himself, to justify his behavior in any meaningful context spiritually failed. I think that once he started killed the first time, he had a chance at redemption, if he had turned himself in. <BR> <BR>Ironically, I think we all lived through his last chance right here, when &#40;possibly for the first time in history&#41; a killer at large conversed with the general population [to some extent]and we asked him to turn himself in, if he had done so willingly, I would think he was redeemable. However, I&#39;m not buying any &#34;he let himself be caught&#34; crap. That don&#39;t count.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
