<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12354"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 28, 2005 - 1:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno, thank you for that insight. Do you really think he prayed over them? You know, now that I think of it the Lutheran chuch I don&#39;t believe acknowledged hypocracy. Which would make sense in that &#34;your expected to committ such sins&#34;, yet go about denying them to all other faiths as long as you keep it within the chuch. So maybe, there is some verse or hymm or poem written that references this type of hypocracy.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
