<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12358"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 28, 2005 - 3:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Could he have prayed over his victims bodies? Why not, New Jersey murderer John List did after killing his mother, wife and three kids and then relocated to Denver where he became active again in a church as though nothing had happened. He even called his pastor at the Redeemer Evangelical Lutheran Church after the murders to tell him his family was leaving town for an extended visit to North Carolina and left a note for pastor explaining his reasons for killing and how he prayed over their bodies and how he felt for sure that God would forgive him since Christ died for his sins. He then moved to Denver, joined the St. Pauls Lutheran Church and took up a new life. <BR>Anyone who has read any of the New Testament or been exposed to it in church services or bible studies is plainly aware that Christ hated hypocrisy. It was the one thing that freaked out the normaly otherwise gentle preacher than any other human act. He lashed out against the Pharises for praying publically in the market place and for turning a temple into a marketplace he became physically violent, overturning tables and chasing out the vendors. He asked a crowd ready to stone a woman to death to cast the first stone if any had never sinned. The Bible is full of examples of Christ dealing with hypocrisy. <BR>If Dennis Rader lived during Christ&#39;s lifetime Jesus may have forgave him if was an ignorant heathen athiest murderer but seeing Rader hide behind the church as he kills would have probably propelled Jesus to smack a fist into Rader&#39;s chin. <BR>What would Jesus do? Jesus would have decked him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12358-->
<!--Post: 12361--><!--Time: 1112081795-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12361"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 28, 2005 - 11:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I suspect the BTK Strangler used poetry to appear intelligent to the police, the media and the public. The poetry submitted by the perpetrator was questionable. It was never worthy of the Nobel Prize for Literature. Poetry suggests the writer possesses the capacity for deep thought and eloquent expression. The BTK Strangler intended to achieve it, unfortunately his writing revealed the psyche of a sadistic psychopath.  <BR> <BR>It seems the sobriquet of the BTK Strangler was appropriate. The perpetrator suggested the code name for him would be &#34;Bind them, Torture them, Kill them.&#34; BTK refers to the perpetrator&#39;s modus operandi and the term Strangler refers to the aspect of the murders that provided him with gratification. I believe the suspect enjoyed viewing the victims final breath and the impact of his violence on their body.   <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12361-->
<!--Post: 12391--><!--Time: 1112155406-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12391"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 29, 2005 - 8:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno - are you saying although we are supposed to turn the other cheek and pray for troubled souls, Jesus would have punched DTDC in the head anyway. LOL! Really, that is funny! Ok, now on the subject I noticed the movie psycho was released during DTDC&#39;s hey day, was there a movie soundtrack released with it? Oh by the way I was watching O Brother where art thou the other day, and the word propension and one of BTK&#39;s poems were used in it. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12391-->
<!--Post: 12400--><!--Time: 1112161760-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12400"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 29, 2005 - 9:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky, I&#39;m sorry, but Psycho was released in the early 60&#39;s. Rader would have only just been out of high school &#40;1960 or 61&#41; and that was back in a day when movie soundtracks weren&#39;t always readily available as albums. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12400-->
<!--Post: 12401--><!--Time: 1112164638-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12401"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 29, 2005 - 10:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Foxfire - thanks for the lyrics.  Wow. <BR> <BR>B-mod - I&#39;m definitely following along, and I think you&#39;re right on. <BR> <BR>Tallulabay - extremely interesting stuff.  Now I know why you Zodiac folks find Zodiac so fascinating.  I&#39;m trying to resist....and it&#39;s increasingly futile! <BR> <BR>Johno - I&#39;ve been trying to figure out WWJD, and I think you&#39;ve got it.  I know my own hypocrisy is my biggest curse, but I never considered Rader&#39;s.  I won&#39;t say it&#39;s bigger than mine, but it&#39;s big.  His pastor has one heck of a job cut out for him.   <BR> <BR>Rocky - what a catch!  When everybody talked about that movie popularizing &#34;O Death&#34;, I never considered that there was any more of a connection than the Coen brothers having good taste and one hell of an artistic talent.  But hey, they did Fargo.  They&#39;re true crime buffs.  They&#39;ve got the same &#34;propension&#34; as us.  You got it - they put BTK&#39;s song on the map.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12401-->
<!--Post: 12665--><!--Time: 1112717065-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12665"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 9:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well guys here is something interesting as well, and it&#39;s related to Jethro Thull I found at Kansas.com <BR> <BR>Why was the name &#34;Anderson&#34; in the &#34;Chapter 8&#34; puzzle?  <BR> <BR>Maybe because the name &#34;Larry Anderson&#34; also appeared on one of the fake IDs included in that package to KAKE. But if you include the three letters in front of that surname in the puzzle, it spells out &#34;Ian Anderson.&#34; That&#39;s the name of the lead singer, songwriter and flautist of Jethro Tull. Was BTK a fan of the British band? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12665-->
<!--Post: 12676--><!--Time: 1112743124-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12676"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 4:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well I doubt that suspect Rader was a Tull fan. That&#39;s pretty good music and since we know that Rader as late as 8th grade didn&#39;t even like music I don&#39;t see him as a Tull fan. <BR>I&#39;m sure it merely ment, I &#40; I am&#41; Anderson. <BR>Of course Jethro Tull has a lot of heavy lyrics and songs with creepy lyrics and titles that might appeal to a BTK like Beastie, Crossword, The Curse, Doctor To My Disease, Dogs In The Midwinter, Hymn 43, I Am Your Gun, Mayhen In The High Degree, Old Ghosts, and Occasional Demons to name a few. <BR>I&#39;m sure now he feels Too Old To Rock &#39;n&#39; Roll: Too Young To Die.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12676-->
<!--Post: 12677--><!--Time: 1112748648-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12677"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 5:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t know Johno, do you have any of their music lyrics? Jethro Tull was a lil before my time. I do remember my brother when he got out of the Navy listening to Jethro Tull, then he enlighted me with Nazareth, Beatles, Deep Purple and BTO. &#40; My mom really liked Hair of the Dog and Takin Care of Business&#41;HA! &#40; Ok - me too!&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12677-->
<!--Post: 12679--><!--Time: 1112755317-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12679"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 7:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="Http://remus.rutgers.edu/JethroTull/songs.html" target=_top>Http://remus.rutgers.edu/JethroTull/songs.html</a>  <BR> <BR>Over 270 songs there. <BR>Maybe his poetic inpiration is in there somewhere.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12679-->
<!--Post: 12681--><!--Time: 1112764187-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12681"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 10:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Guys, here&#39;s my problem with Denny liking Tull...it&#39;s often the purview of Ren Fair goers, Tolkien authorities and people who just plain think that the flute <b><u>ROCKS</u></b>.  <BR> <BR>And he <b>did</b> express that &#34;I&#39;m too important for such nonsense&#34; type of sentiment on that form for his 8th grade reunion where music was concerned. My point? Tull is not just rock, but it is arguably <i>intellectual</i> rock, and that goes completely against everything I&#39;ve ever surmised and a lot of what we now know about this tool. I find it much, much easier to believe he&#39;d find old Appalachian folk music interesting. BTK wanted to appear smarter than everyone else, yes, but Denny Rader was not a man who wanted to appear to be a pointy-headed intellectual. Take it from me, since I am kinda pointy-headed. <BR> <BR>And hey, it was never about the music, if you ask me, just the lyrics, and they were all just tools. That&#39;s what people miss, I think - Denny used old folksongs, elizabethan poetry, wordfinds from Games books, as tools to further his secondary goal, which was humiliate the police. <BR> <BR>Dual motivations, think about it. This guy had a <u>degree in criminal justice</u>, y&#39;all. And the <b>cops wouldn&#39;t take him in.</b> He had to <i>settle.</i> If you&#39;ve read Beattie&#39;s book, for instance, it has the entire text of the letter where BTK names himself. Not only does he give himself the moniker, but the rest of the letter is a <u>lecture</u> to the police - he seemed incredulous that they hadn&#39;t connected his crimes. He seemed basically to intend this message - &#34;hey, idiots, here I am, come get me, nyahnyah..&#34; <BR> <BR>The man&#39;s primary motivation for murder was his own sexual gratification. His primary motivation for the lousily constructed wordfind, the really awful poetry, the moronic-sounding letters, was to humiliate the men and women chasing him.  <BR> <BR>Jethro Tull as one of his sources just seems a stretch to me, incongruous to the public and private man and killer. The more I examine this, the more I see an odd consistency to Dennis Rader&#39;s personality - that was why some things I was convinced about where he was concerned were correct - BTK was obviously compulsively neat - my God, he swept the glass he broke at Nancy Fox&#39;s house into a &#34;tidy pile&#34; after he got in. Nearly every letter Beattie reprinted in &#34;Nightmare&#34; mentions something about the neatness of the victim&#39;s homes, with only Nancy Fox getting a passing grade. Bizarre, I know, but now that we know what a completely anal-retentive ninny Dennis Rader was known to be, it makes sense. Tull would be incongruous as a choice of music for him. Hell, if Denny listened to music at all in any casual way outside of what he may have encountered in college, I&#39;d be surprised.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12681-->
<!--Post: 12683--><!--Time: 1112768509-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12683"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 11:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->We know he was exposed somehow to the old Apalachian tradional folk song O&#39; Death because he uses it to recreate his own Oh Death to Nancy poem. <BR>We also know he grew up in a religious household and as an adult became a regular church goer and married into a family that helped found a church so all his life he is exposed to Hymnal music in his church and the text of the Bible. <BR>He apparently grows up with none or little interest in popular music like Rock, Country, R&amp;B or whatever. <BR>We don&#39;t know of him as having any interest in literature or excelling at it school or college and we know he didn&#39;t major in it. <BR>So again, what would cause him to began mimicking known poetry and writing his own poetry and then go so far as to nomimnate as a contending moniker for himself the name The Poetic Strangler? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12683-->
<!--Post: 12699--><!--Time: 1112844311-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12699"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 8:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey - Maybe his Grandpa or someone he knew played the fiddle. Remember that clue.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12699-->
<!--Post: 12715--><!--Time: 1112898819-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12715"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lizzie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lizzie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 07, 2005 - 11:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->he was able to re-live the acts and thoughts through the poems and I&#39;m sure he thinks he did a brilliant job, I would have preferred he recreate Dr. Seuss books myself. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12715-->
</table></td></tr></table>
